// How to give part of a program memory of its own: another arena, and a
// block run on it.
//
// The program's arena — `#allocator Arena_Basic(16 * MB)`, at the top of the
// module main is in — is where values of 1 KB or more go (see arenas.mara).
// Another arena of its kind is a value: `level := Arena_Basic(4 * MB)`.
//
// `with &level {…}` (or `with &level do …`) runs its block on it: what the
// block puts on an arena, and what everything it calls puts there, goes on
// level. At every way out of the block — its end, a return, `?`, a break or
// continue — the arena it was is back, so the code around it never sees
// level. A with inside a with runs on the inner one's.
//
// It's how a job gets memory apart from the rest: a level's, a request's, a
// library's. A DLL's #expose funs run on the arena their host hands them
// (their first param, when the DLL names the kind: `#allocator Arena_Basic`).
// An arena's memory is reserved for as long as the program runs: make one
// for a long-lived job, not one per call.
module with_arena

use mara.core
use mara.memory

#allocator Arena_Basic(16 * MB)

// A level's work: a big buffer — on the arena in use.
load_level :: fun(level: ^Arena_Basic) {
	tiles : [64 * KB]byte
	tiles[0] = u8(1)
	print("loading: the level's arena holds", level.base.len, "bytes")
}

main :: fun() {
	level := Arena_Basic(1 * MB)

	// Run on the level's arena, load_level's buffer goes there — and is gone
	// when load_level ends.
	with &level do load_level(&level)
	print("after: the level's arena holds", level.base.len, "bytes")   // 0
}
